  <!-- 3. PROBLEM SECTION -->
  <section class="sp-problem" aria-labelledby="problem-h2">
    <div class="sp-problem-inner">
      <p class="sp-section-label">Common Issues</p>
      <h2 class="sp-section-h2" id="problem-h2">What Goes Wrong With<br><em>Chimney &amp; Fireplace Masonry</em></h2>
      <p class="sp-section-sub">A chimney is one of the most structurally exposed masonry elements on your home. Cut corners here and the consequences are safety issues — not just repair costs.</p>
      <div class="sp-problem-grid">
        <div class="sp-problem-card">
          <div class="sp-problem-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#D4A931" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 22s8-4 8-10V5l-8-3-8 3v7c0 6 8 10 8 10z"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
          </div>
          <p class="sp-problem-title">No Cricket = Water Damage</p>
          <p class="sp-problem-body">A chimney cricket (a small ridge diverting water around the chimney) is required on all chimneys over 30 inches wide. Skip it and you get guaranteed water intrusion at the chimney-roof junction — the most common source of attic leaks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-problem-card">
          <div class="sp-problem-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#D4A931" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><line x1="12" y1="8" x2="12" y2="12"/><line x1="12" y1="16" x2="12.01" y2="16"/></svg>
          </div>
          <p class="sp-problem-title">Wrong Crown = Premature Failure</p>
          <p class="sp-problem-body">The chimney crown seals the top of the flue. A thin, flat crown cracks within 2–3 winters. A proper crown is sloped, overhangs the brick face, and is cast in a concrete mix — not mortar.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-problem-card">
          <div class="sp-problem-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#D4A931" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M8.5 14.5A2.5 2.5 0 0 0 11 12c0-1.38-.5-2-1-3-1.072-2.143-.224-4.054 2-6 .5 2.5 2 4.9 4 6.5 2 1.6 3 3.5 3 5.5a7 7 0 1 1-14 0c0-1.153.433-2.294 1-3a2.5 2.5 0 0 0 2.5 2.5z"/></svg>
          </div>
          <p class="sp-problem-title">No Refractory Mortar in Firebox</p>
          <p class="sp-problem-body">Standard mortar fails at firebox temperatures — cracking joints, allowing heat transfer to combustibles, and creating fire hazard. Refractory mortar is required; it's not optional.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-problem-card">
          <div class="sp-problem-icon" aria-hidden="true">
            <svg width="32" height="32" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#D4A931" stroke-width="2" aria-hidden="true"><rect x="3" y="3" width="18" height="18" rx="2"/><path d="M9 9h6M9 12h6M9 15h4"/></svg>
          </div>
          <p class="sp-problem-title">Improper Clearances = Code Failure</p>
          <p class="sp-problem-body">Fireplaces and chimneys have minimum clearances from combustibles — inspectors check these on every visit. Non-compliant clearances require full rebuild; there's no patching your way to compliance.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. PROCESS SECTION -->
  <section class="sp-process" aria-labelledby="process-h2">
    <div class="sp-process-inner">
      <p class="sp-section-label">How We Work</p>
      <h2 class="sp-section-h2" id="process-h2">Our Chimney &amp; Fireplace<br><em>Process</em></h2>
      <p class="sp-section-sub">From drawing review to final walk-through — code-compliant construction that passes inspection the first time.</p>
      <div class="sp-process-steps" role="list">
        <div class="sp-step" role="listitem">
          <span class="sp-step-num" aria-hidden="true">01</span>
          <div class="sp-step-content">
            <p class="sp-step-title">Drawing Review &amp; Code Check</p>
            <p class="sp-step-body">We review your drawings against current IRC and local code — chimney height, clearances, flue sizing, and crown specifications — before any work begins.</p>
            <p class="sp-step-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Chimney construction errors discovered at inspection mean rebuild. We catch them on paper first.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-step" role="listitem">
          <span class="sp-step-num" aria-hidden="true">02</span>
          <div class="sp-step-content">
            <p class="sp-step-title">Foundation &amp; Footing Inspection</p>
            <p class="sp-step-body">Chimney mass requires an engineered footing. We verify footing capacity and dimensions before constructing the chimney base.</p>
            <p class="sp-step-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> An underbuilt footing settles — and the chimney with it. Settling chimneys crack, separate from the firebox, and eventually become a structural hazard.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-step" role="listitem">
          <span class="sp-step-num" aria-hidden="true">03</span>
          <div class="sp-step-content">
            <p class="sp-step-title">Brick or Block Construction</p>
            <p class="sp-step-body">We construct the chimney in planned courses — checking plumb at every lift, using correct mortar type for each application (standard for exterior, refractory for firebox and smoke chamber).</p>
            <p class="sp-step-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Plumb checking at every lift prevents cumulative lean. A chimney that's 1/4" off per course is visibly crooked at 20 feet and may fail inspection.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-step" role="listitem">
          <span class="sp-step-num" aria-hidden="true">04</span>
          <div class="sp-step-content">
            <p class="sp-step-title">Crown, Cap &amp; Flashing Installation</p>
            <p class="sp-step-body">We pour or form a proper sloped crown, install a code-compliant cap, and flash the chimney-roof junction to current standards — including a cricket where required.</p>
            <p class="sp-step-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Crown, cap, and flashing are the three components that protect the chimney from water. All three must be done correctly. Homeowners find out they weren't when the attic leaks.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-step" role="listitem">
          <span class="sp-step-num" aria-hidden="true">05</span>
          <div class="sp-step-content">
            <p class="sp-step-title">Firebox Completion &amp; Final Inspection</p>
            <p class="sp-step-body">We complete the firebox with refractory mortar, install damper, and conduct a final walk-through including flue visual check.</p>
            <p class="sp-step-why"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> The firebox is the safety-critical component. Every installation is walked before we close out — we won't hand over a fireplace we wouldn't use in our own home.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    (function() {
      function initStepAnimations() {
        var gsap = window.__gsap;
        var ScrollTrigger = window.__ScrollTrigger;
        if (!gsap || !ScrollTrigger) return;
        var steps = document.querySelectorAll('.sp-step');
        if (!steps.length) return;
        steps.forEach(function(step) {
          gsap.fromTo(step, { opacity: 0, x: -24 }, {
            opacity: 1, x: 0, duration: 0.55, ease: 'power2.out',
            scrollTrigger: { trigger: step, start: 'top 88%', toggleActions: 'play none none reverse' }
          });
        });
      }
      // Always wait for animations:ready — prevents race with BaseLayout ScrollTrigger.kill()
      window.removeEventListener('animations:ready', window.__spStepAnimReady);
      window.__spStepAnimReady = initStepAnimations;
      window.addEventListener('animations:ready', window.__spStepAnimReady);
      if (window.__lenis) initStepAnimations();
    })();
  </script>

