  <section class="sp-faq" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <div class="sp-faq-inner">
      <p class="sp-section-label">FAQ</p>
      <h2 class="sp-section-h2" id="faq-h2">Chimney &amp; Fireplace FAQ<br><em>Answered Honestly</em></h2>
      <div class="sp-faq-list" role="list">
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-1" id="faq-q-1">
            Do I need a permit to build or rebuild a chimney?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-1" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-1">
            <p>Yes — chimney construction and major repair requires a permit in most Chicagoland municipalities. AMS pulls all required permits, coordinates inspections, and maintains a clean inspection record.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-2" id="faq-q-2">
            How long does chimney construction take?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-2" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-2">
            <p>A new chimney construction takes 5–10 days depending on height and complexity. Chimney rebuild from the roofline takes 1–3 days. A fireplace surround installation runs 2–3 days.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-3" id="faq-q-3">
            What is refractory mortar and why does it matter?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-3" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-3">
            <p>Refractory mortar is a heat-resistant mortar designed for fireplace temperatures (2,000°F+). Standard mortar fails at these temperatures — cracking joints, allowing heat to transfer to combustibles. Every AMS firebox uses refractory mortar, not standard mortar.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-4" id="faq-q-4">
            What is a chimney cricket?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-4" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-4">
            <p>A cricket is a small ridged structure installed behind a chimney to divert water around it rather than collecting at the chimney-roof junction. It's required by code on all chimneys over 30 inches wide. AMS installs crickets on every applicable chimney.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-5" id="faq-q-5">
            Can you match existing brick on a chimney rebuild?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-5" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-5">
            <p>Yes — we source brick to match your existing chimney and exterior brick in color and texture. The goal is a rebuild that's visually indistinguishable from the original.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-6" id="faq-q-6">
            Is AMS licensed for fireplace and chimney construction?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-6" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-6">
            <p>Yes — licensed masonry contractor in Illinois (License #MA-104-018887), $2.5M liability, workers' comp. We pull permits and work above board on every job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-7" id="faq-q-7">
            What is a chimney crown and why does it fail?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-7" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-7">
            <p>The crown is the concrete or mortar cap that seals the top of the chimney around the flue. Thin or flat crowns crack within 2–3 Chicago winters from freeze-thaw cycles. A proper crown is sloped, overhangs the brick face, and is cast in concrete — not mortar. AMS builds code-compliant crowns only.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="sp-faq-item" role="listitem">
          <button class="sp-faq-q" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-a-8" id="faq-q-8">
            How do I know if my chimney needs repair vs. full rebuild?
            <svg class="sp-faq-icon" width="18" height="18" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" aria-hidden="true"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="sp-faq-a" id="faq-a-8" role="region" aria-labelledby="faq-q-8">
            <p>If deterioration is limited to mortar joints, crown, or cap — repair is typically appropriate. If the brick itself is spalling or the structure has moved, a partial or full rebuild may be necessary. AMS assesses on site and gives you an honest recommendation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <script is:inline>
    (function() {
      var btns = document.querySelectorAll('.sp-faq-q');
      btns.forEach(function(btn) {
        btn.addEventListener('click', function() {
          var expanded = this.getAttribute('aria-expanded') === 'true';
          btns.forEach(function(b) {
            b.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'false');
            var ans = document.getElementById(b.getAttribute('aria-controls'));
            if (ans) ans.style.maxHeight = null;
          });
          if (!expanded) {
            this.setAttribute('aria-expanded', 'true');
            var answer = document.getElementById(this.getAttribute('aria-controls'));
            if (answer) answer.style.maxHeight = answer.scrollHeight + 'px';
          }
        });
        btn.addEventListener('keydown', function(e) {
          if (e.key === 'Enter' || e.key === ' ') { e.preventDefault(); this.click(); }
        });
      });
    })();
  </script>

